Abstract = {Community uptake of knowledge about the environment, perception of the
   environment, and environmental awareness are of pivotal importance.
   Secondary school students represent an emerging young generation that
   will become actively involved in shaping the discourse of our societies,
   as they face major issues such as climate change and biodiversity loss.
   The aim of the study was to explore environmental awareness and
   attitudes in relation to environmental policies on the national and
   European level. The study was conducted on the secondary school students
   from the Czech Republic and the Slovak Republic and differences between
   the two countries and gender were investigated. Based on a
   questionnaire, the acquired data suggest that secondary school students
   exhibit environmental awareness and display environmentally friendly
   behaviour, by which they aim to reduce their negative burden on the
   environment. The study group identified environmental pollution and
   climate change as the most relevant contributors to this global crisis,
   while excessive construction, biodiversity loss, and insufficient
   protection were identified to a lesser degree. The environment as a
   whole was perceived as endangered, among which soil was singularly the
   least pronounced environmental category. The results show a strong
   negative perception of national actions to protect the environment, and
   the secondary school students in the study supported the idea that
   countries should be doing more to protect the environment. The European
   Union's ambitious plans to reach carbon neutrality in 2050 were also met
   with a degree of pessimism and uncertainty.},
